{% extends 'guides/how_long_by_pieces/_layout.html.twig' %}

{% block size_intro %}
    <p>
        A 100-piece jigsaw is the sprint of the puzzle world. It is the classic size for children's puzzles and a quick
        family activity — and for adults, the quickest puzzle you are likely to time, short enough to do several in one
        evening. Here is how long it really takes, measured instead of guessed.
    </p>
{% endblock %}

{% block size_callout %}
    <div class="guide-callout">
        <p>
            With a median of {{ distribution.medianSeconds|compactTime }}, the setup is a real part of the time: turning
            100 pieces face-up takes the same minute or two however fast you build. If you want a faster time, flip and
            pull out the edge pieces in one pass instead of two.
        </p>
    </div>
{% endblock %}

{% block size_factors %}
    <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
            <strong>Piece size.</strong> Children's 100-piece puzzles often use extra-large pieces. They are easy to see
            and pick up, but the finished picture can be as big as a 500-piece puzzle, so you reach further for every
            piece.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Bold pictures.</strong> Many 100-piece puzzles show cartoon or character art with clear blocks of
            colour, and those go together fast. A 100-piece photo of sky, sea or a single-colour pattern is a different
            puzzle altogether, even though the piece count is the same.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hands, not planning.</strong> With so few pieces there is little to sort. Most of the time goes into
            picking up, trying and placing, so a clear table and pieces within reach matter more than any strategy.
        </li>
    </ul>
{% endblock %}
